This is all the needed information in order to compare Stokes constants associated to the partition function and to the free energy. In fact, \eqref{eq:Stokes-aut-zero-v1} and \eqref{eq:Stokes-aut-zero-v2} should yield the same result, as should \eqref{eq:Stokes-aut-pi-v1} and \eqref{eq:Stokes-aut-pi-v2}. Making sure such identifications hold will automatically compute the Stokes constants. For instance, from \eqref{eq:Stokes-aut-zero-v2} follows  egin{equation} \underline{\mathfrak{S}}_{0} F (x, 	au_1, 	au_2) = F \left(x, 	au_1+S_{1}^{F}, 	au_{2}ight), \end{equation} 
oindent which, when compared to \eqref{eq:Stokes-aut-zero-v1}, immediately yields the Stokes constant $S_{1}^{F}$  egin{equation} S_{1}^{F} = S_{1}^{Z} \quad (\, = - 2\, ). \end{equation} For the second comparison, that of \eqref{eq:Stokes-aut-pi-v1} and \eqref{eq:Stokes-aut-pi-v2}, the action of the exponential in \eqref{eq:Stokes-aut-pi-v2} is now more intricate and we begin by expanding it as ootnote{Note that $\widetilde{S}_{-k}^F\, 	au_1^k  rac{\partial}{\partial	au_2}$ only acts once upon the transseries, as its $	au_2$-dependence is purely linear.} (using the explicit transseries in, 	extit{e.g.}, \eqref{eq:Free-en-two-param-transs})  egin{eqnarray} \exp \left\{ \sum_{k=1}^{+\infty} \left( S_{-k}^{F}\, 	au_1^{k+1}  rac{\partial}{\partial	au_1} + \widetilde{S}_{-k}^F\, 	au_1^k  rac{\partial}{\partial	au_2} ight) ight\} F (x, 	au_{1}, 	au_{2}) &=& 	au_2 + \sum_{\ell=1}^{+\infty}  rac{1}{\ell!} \left( \sum_{k=1}^{+\infty} S_{-k}^{F}\, 	au_1^{k+1}  rac{\partial}{\partial	au_1} ight)^{\ell-1} 	imes 
onumber \ && \hspace{-200pt} 	imes \sum_{k'=1}^{+\infty}\widetilde{S}_{-k'}^F\, 	au_1^{k'} + \sum_{n=0}^{+\infty} \sum_{\ell=0}^{+\infty}  rac{1}{\ell!} \left( \sum_{k=1}^{+\infty} S_{-k}^{F}\, 	au_1^{k+1}  rac{\partial}{\partial	au_1}ight)^{\ell} 	au_1^n\, {\mathrm{e}}^{- rac{n A}{x}}\, \Phi_{(n,0)} (x). \end{eqnarray} 
oindent Clearly, in order to compare back to the resulting (shifted) transseries in \eqref{eq:Stokes-aut-pi-v1}, we will also need to expand that expression. One simply obtains  egin{eqnarray} F \left(x,  rac{	au_1}{1 + S_{-1}^{Z} 	au_{1}}, 	au_{2} + \log \left( 1 + S_{-1}^{Z} 	au_{1} ight) ight) &=& \ && \hspace{-100pt} = 	au_2 + \log \left( 1 + S_{-1}^{Z} 	au_{1} ight) + \sum_{n=0}^{+\infty} \left(  rac{	au_1}{1 + S_{-1}^{Z} 	au_{1}} ight)^n {\mathrm{e}}^{- rac{n A}{x}}\, F^{(n)} (x). 
onumber \end{eqnarray} 
oindent The above two expressions will match if, upon the standard identification \eqref{transseries-Phin0-vs-Fn}, one further has  egin{eqnarray} \log \left( 1 + S_{-1}^{Z} 	au_{1} ight) &=& \sum_{k=1}^{+\infty} \widetilde{S}_{-k}^F \left(\, \sum_{\ell=1}^{+\infty}  rac{1}{\ell!} \left\{ \sum_{k'=1}^{+\infty} S_{-k'}^{F}\, 	au_1^{k'+1}  rac{\partial}{\partial	au_1} ight\}^{\ell-1}\, ight) 	au_1^{k}, \  rac{	au_1^n}{\left( 1 + S_{-1}^{Z} 	au_{1} ight)^n} &=& \left(\, \sum_{\ell=0}^{+\infty}  rac{1}{\ell!} \left\{ \sum_{k=1}^{+\infty} S_{-k}^{F}\, 	au_1^{k+1}  rac{\partial}{\partial	au_1} ight\}^{\ell}\, ight) 	au_1^n, \qquad  orall n\ge1. \end{eqnarray} 
oindent While at first these may seem as intricate conditions, we shall see in the following that there is a very simple set of values for the Stokes constants $S_{-k}^F$ and $\widetilde{S}_{-k}^F$, $k\ge1$, which solve both conditions. Let us exemplify how this works starting with \eqref{Stokes-comp-2nd} and computing the $S_{-k}^F$. For $n=1$, a power-series expansion of the left-hand side, together with the recursive action of the differential operator on the right-hand side, leads to  egin{eqnarray} \sum_{\ell=0}^{+\infty} \left(-S_{-1}^{Z}ight)^{\ell} 	au_{1}^{\ell+1} & = & 	au_{1} + \sum_{k_{1}=1}^{+\infty} S_{-k_{1}}^{F}\, 	au_{1}^{k_{1}+1} +  rac{1}{2!} \sum_{k_{1},k_{2}=1}^{+\infty} \left( k_{1}+1 ight) S_{-k_{1}}^{F} S_{-k_{2}}^{F}\, 	au_{1}^{k_{1}+k_{2}+1} + \ &+&  rac{1}{3!} \sum_{k_{1},k_{2},k_{3}=1}^{+\infty} \left( k_{1}+1 ight) \left( k_{1}+k_{2}+1 ight) S_{-k_{1}}^{F} S_{-k_{2}}^{F} S_{-k_{3}}^{F}\, 	au_{1}^{\sum_{i=1}^{3}k_{i}+1} + \cdots. 
onumber \end{eqnarray} 
oindent Let us compare equal powers of $	au_1^{\ell+1}$. For $\ell=0$ we get an identity; for the next few $\ell$'s we find  egin{eqnarray} \ell = 1: & -S_{-1}^{Z} & = S_{-1}^{F}, \ \ell = 2: & \left( -S_{-1}^{Z} ight)^{2} & = S_{-2}^{F} +  rac{1}{2!} \left( 1+1 ight) \left( S_{-1}^{F} ight)^{2}, \ \ell = 3: & \left( -S_{-1}^{Z} ight)^{3} & = S_{-3}^{F} +  rac{1}{2!} \left( 2+3 ight) S_{-1}^{F} S_{-2}^{F} +  rac{1}{3!} \left( 2 	imes 3 ight) \left( S_{-1}^{F} ight)^{3}. \end{eqnarray} 
oindent The first equation above, \eqref{Stokes-comp-one}, immediately yields $S_{-1}^{F} = -S_{-1}^{Z}$. Recursively solving the following equations one sees that \eqref{Stokes-comp-two} then yields $S_{-2}^{F} =0$ and \eqref{Stokes-comp-three} $S_{-3}^{F} =0$. This is a pattern which generalizes to higher powers of $	au_1$. Indeed, the first time the Stokes constant $S_{-k}^F$ will enter the game is when we write down the equation associated to $\ell=k$. But, at this stage, such equation will also include a term just in $\left(S_{-1}^Fight)^k$, while all other terms will be products of Stokes constants which always include at least one ootnote{The $m$-term contribution to the sum in \eqref{Stokes-comp-2nd} is given by  egin{equation}  rac{1}{m!} \prod_{i=1}^{m} \left(\, \sum_{k_{i}=1}^{+\infty} \left(\, \sum_{j=1}^{i-1}k_{j}+1\, ight) S_{-k_{i}}^{F}\, ight) 	au_{1}^{\sum_{j=1}^{m}k_{j}+1}. \end{equation} } $S_{-k'}^F$ with $1<k'<k$. For a general $\ell$ this looks like  egin{equation} \left( -S_{-1}^{Z} ight)^{\ell} = S_{-\ell}^{F} + \cdots +  rac{1}{\ell!}\, \ell!\left( S_{-1}^{F} ight)^{\ell}, \end{equation} 
oindent where the dots indicate terms involving Stokes constants $S_{-k}^F$, $1<k<\ell$, which were already set to zero from previous conditions. By induction, it follows that $S_{-k}^F=0$, for all $k>1$. Finally, it is straightforward to show that this solution also solves the initial constraint \eqref{Stokes-comp-2nd} for any other value of $n\ge1$. This is indeed the case as  egin{equation} \sum_{\ell=0}^{+\infty}  rac{1}{\ell!} \left( S_{-1}^{F} ight)^{\ell} \left( 	au_{1}^{2}  rac{\partial}{\partial	au_{1}} ight)^{\ell} 	au_{1}^{n} = 	au_{1}^{n}\, \sum_{\ell=0}^{+\infty}  rac{\left( n + \ell - 1 ight)!}{\ell! \left( n - 1 ight)!} \left( S_{-1}^{F} 	au_{1} ight)^{\ell} =  rac{	au_{1}^{n}}{\left( 1 - S_{-1}^{F} 	au_{1} ight)^{n}}. \end{equation} 
oindent A completely analogous procedure can be used to solve the remaining constraint \eqref{Stokes-comp-1st} (where one also has to use the above solution for the $S_{-k}^F$), which we leave as an exercise for the reader.

	extbf{Large-order relations:} The large-order (large $k$) behaviour of the coefficients $F_k^{(n,0)}$, associated to the node $\Phi_{(n,0)}$, is given by a 	extit{sum} over 	extit{all} forward 	extit{and} backward paths, linking nodes to the right $\Phi_{(m>n,0)}$ and to the left $\Phi_{(m<n,0)}$, 	extit{in addition} to a sum over paths to the extra ``orthogonal'' node in the chain, $\Phi_{(0,1)}$. \vspace{10pt} Each term in this sum ($\Phi_{(n,0)} ightarrow \Phi_{(m,\ell=0,1)}$) can be decomposed into three factors:  egin{itemize} \item Leading growth-factor, dictated solely by the order $k$ of the coefficient:  egin{equation*}  rac{\Gamma(k)}{2\pi{\mathrm{i}}\, A^k}. \end{equation*} 
oindent \item Large-order factor \eqref{large-order-factor}, dictated by beginning and end nodes:  egin{equation*} \chi_{(nightarrow m)}(k). \end{equation*} 
oindent \item Statistical factor, sum over all the allowed paths $\mathcal{P}(n ightarrow m)$ linking the nodes as in figure~ef{fig:first-5-sector-alien-one-point-five}:  egin{equation*} \mathrm{SF}_{(n ightarrow m)} \equiv \sum_{\mathcal{P}(n ightarrow m)} \mathrm{CF} ({\cal P})\, w (\mathcal{P}). \end{equation*} \end{itemize} \vspace{-5pt} }} \vspace{15pt} 
oindent Spelled out in writing, the result is:  egin{equation} F_k^{(n,0)}\,  rac{2\pi{\mathrm{i}}\, A^k}{\Gamma(k)} \simeq \sum_{m
e n} \mathrm{SF}_{(nightarrow m)}\, \chi_{(nightarrow m)}(k). \end{equation}

Indeed, let us consider the partition function of some scalar quantum field theory,  egin{equation} Z (\lambda) = \int \left[ {\mathrm{d}} \varphi ight] {\mathrm{e}}^{- S(\varphi)}, \end{equation} 
oindent with action $S(\varphi)$ and coupling-constant $\lambda$. In order to use the toolbox described in this section, one would first want to factor out the coupling constant, as in  egin{equation} S (\varphi) 	o  rac{1}{\lambda} S (\widetilde{\varphi} ), \end{equation} 
oindent and then compute (functional) saddle points for the functional-integral partition function \eqref{eq:path_integral}. But this factoring can only be done when considering 	extit{bare} quantities. As renormalization kicks in, the coupling runs as $\lambda \equiv \lambda (\mu)$ at energy-scale $\mu$, further inducing mass renormalization and wave-function renormalization. For our purposes, the overall effect of such renormalization procedure is to 	extit{invalidate} the above factoring. One rather finds (very schematically---see \cite{k16} for a full discussion)  egin{equation} S (\varphi) 	o  rac{1}{\lambda} S_{-1} (\widetilde{\varphi} ) + S_{0} (\widetilde{\varphi} ) + \lambda S_{1} (\widetilde{\varphi} ) + \lambda^2 S_{2} (\widetilde{\varphi} ) + \cdots. \end{equation} 
oindent This automatically implies that in order to address renormalizable quantum field theories, one will need more sophisticated saddle-point methods and answers than just \eqref{eq:thimble_asymp}, and likely more complicated transseries structures than the ones we have been addressing so far (see section~ef{sec:physics} or, 	extit{e.g.}, \cite{e08}, for some such structures; and \cite{acpy17} for recent results pointing towards the need to include some of them within Yang--Mills/QCD theories).

Let us check that this is in fact verified. Start with the asymptotic expansion for $\Phi_0 (\lambda)$, in \eqref{coeff-quartic}, which we reproduce in here  egin{equation} \Phi_{0} (\lambda) \simeq \sum_{k=0}^{+\infty} Z^{(0)}_k\, \lambda^k, \qquad Z^{(0)}_k = \left(  rac{2}{3} ight)^k  rac{(4k)!}{2^{6k}\, (2k)!\, k!}\, Z^{(0)}_0, \end{equation} 
oindent and where in the present conventions $Z^{(0)}_0 =  rac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}}$. Computing the Borel transform above, using \eqref{eq:borelwithsquareroot}, it follows  egin{equation} {\cal B}[\sqrt{\lambda}\, \Phi_0](s) = \sum_{k=0}^{+\infty} Z^{(0)}_k\,  rac{s^{k- rac{1}{2}}}{\Gamma \left( k+ rac{1}{2} ight)} =  rac{1}{2\pi \sqrt{s}}\,  rac{\sqrt{1+\sqrt{1- rac{2s}{3}}}}{\sqrt{1- rac{2s}{3}}}, \end{equation} 
oindent which is precisely ${\cal B}[\Psi_0](s)$ as expected. Performing the same analysis using the asymptotic expansion for $\Phi_1 (\lambda)$, in \eqref{coeff-quartic-inst}, one likewise obtains  egin{equation} {\cal B}[\sqrt{\lambda}\, \Phi_1](s) = \sum_{k=0}^{+\infty} Z^{(1)}_k\,  rac{s^{k- rac{1}{2}}}{\Gamma \left( k+ rac{1}{2} ight)} = -  rac{{\mathrm{i}}}{2\pi \sqrt{2s}}\,  rac{\sqrt{1+\sqrt{1+ rac{2s}{3}}}}{\sqrt{1+ rac{2s}{3}}}, \end{equation} 
oindent which precisely matches ${\cal B}[\Psi_1](s)$. This explicitly checks our calculations, verifying how there is more than one way to compute a Borel transform, leading to 	extit{distinct, albeit related} results.

The first thing to notice is that the Borel transforms \eqref{eq:Borel-Z0-after-change-var} and \eqref{eq:Borel-Z1-after-change-var} both have a square-root singularity at the origin, 	extit{i.e.}, $\sim 1/\sqrt{s}$ near $s=0$. This is a somewhat artificial singularity from the resurgence standpoint, and is essentially related to the coupling square-root in the initial asymptotic expansion. In order to infer whether there is any clean resurgent behaviour in our ``new'' Borel transforms, we need to analyze them away from this singularity. In fact, we shall need to focus on the singularity at $s= rac{3}{2}$ of the Borel transform ${\cal B}[\Psi_0](s)$, and on the singularity at $s=- rac{3}{2}$ of the Borel transform ${\cal B}[\Psi_1](s)$. If resurgence is also at work in here, near each of these singularities we should see the ``reappearance'' of the other sector. Expanding the Borel transforms \eqref{eq:Borel-Z0-after-change-var} and \eqref{eq:Borel-Z1-after-change-var} near their singularities at $s = \pm A \equiv \pm  rac{3}{2}$, we find  egin{eqnarray} {\cal B}[\Psi_0](s) &=& \sum_{k=-1}^{+\infty} B_k^{(0)} \left( s - A ight)^{ rac{k}{2}}, \qquad B_k^{(0)} =  rac{{\mathrm{e}}^{{\mathrm{i}}  rac{k\pi}{2}}}{2\pi^{3/2}}\,  rac{\Gamma \left( k+ rac{3}{2} ight)}{\Gamma \left( k+2 ight)}\, A^{- rac{k+1}{2}}, \ {\cal B}[\Psi_1](s) &=& \sum_{k=-1}^{+\infty} B_k^{(1)} \left( s + A ight)^{ rac{k}{2}}, \qquad B_k^{(1)} =  rac{{\mathrm{e}}^{{\mathrm{i}}\pi}}{\left(2\piight)^{3/2}}\,  rac{\Gamma \left( k+ rac{3}{2} ight)}{\Gamma \left( k+2 ight)}\, A^{- rac{k+1}{2}}. \end{eqnarray} 
